1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,759
Statistical process control is a handful of formulas,

2
00:00:03,859 --> 00:00:06,308
and every one of them is a picture.

3
00:00:06,400 --> 00:00:09,180
Two of those pictures carry the rest.

4
00:00:09,966 --> 00:00:14,635
First idea. A control chart plots subgroup averages in the order

5
00:00:14,735 --> 00:00:19,106
they were made, and its centre line is simply their average.

6
00:00:19,200 --> 00:00:22,902
Let the process talk: thirty six subgroup means,

7
00:00:23,002 --> 00:00:25,676
and the spread they happen to have.

8
00:00:25,766 --> 00:00:29,737
Now widen a band around that centre line and count what

9
00:00:29,837 --> 00:00:34,252
falls outside. By three of those sigmas nothing is, with room

10
00:00:34,352 --> 00:00:38,026
to spare — and that is where the control limits go.

11
00:00:38,116 --> 00:00:43,176
Written out, the upper limit is the grand mean plus three sigma hat.

12
00:00:43,266 --> 00:00:47,534
Which for this process is a number the process itself chose.

13
00:00:47,633 --> 00:00:53,383
Limits come from the data. Specifications come from the customer. Confusing

14
00:00:53,483 --> 00:00:57,517
those two is the most common mistake on a shop floor.

15
00:00:58,816 --> 00:01:02,468
Second idea. Take the same process and plot it

16
00:01:02,568 --> 00:01:07,116
against the measurement itself. This is where parts land.

17
00:01:07,216 --> 00:01:10,328
Then the customer's two limits. These were

18
00:01:10,428 --> 00:01:14,076
not calculated from anything. They were dictated.

19
00:01:14,166 --> 00:01:19,356
Everything past them is scrap, and the near gap decides how

20
00:01:19,456 --> 00:01:24,914
much. Distance to the nearest limit, over three sigma, is Cpk.

21
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:29,628
At this scale the red is a sliver. It

22
00:01:29,728 --> 00:01:34,740
is 1,350 parts per million — one in 741.

23
00:01:34,833 --> 00:01:38,302
So the chart tells you whether the process is steady,

24
00:01:38,402 --> 00:01:42,005
and capability tells you whether steady is good enough.

